INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable SASRec extension. All improvements below are hypotheses; the supplied diagnostics establish neither validation gains nor functional compatibility.

### Insights and directions

**Starting-point insights.** (1) SASRec already provides causal sequence features. A residual extension preserves this useful starting representation. (2) Users report that broad associations displace recent intent. Explicit recent-history features could strengthen evidence for choosing an item now. (3) Repeated narrow preferences are useful, whereas isolated interactions are ambiguous. Retrieving historical evidence with recent intent could distinguish these cases without inventing user traits.

**Related-work insights.** (1) [Hierarchical long/short-term modeling](https://journal.hep.com.cn/fcs/EN/10.1007/s11704-025-41181-y) retrieves persistent interests using recent intent. This motivates lightweight attention over historical items. (2) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates learning against multiple negatives. Electronics’ 14.95% wrong-ranking rate makes separation a reasonable experimental target. (3) [MQSA-TED](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01056) models item transitions. Directional evidence could help distinguish plausible continuation from generic similarity, although transitions cannot establish equipment compatibility.

These insights define three directions: temporal evidence selection, ranking objectives, and directional transitions.

### Framework and candidate assessment

Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each assessment lists a benefit (+) and limitation (−).

| Idea and mechanism | Scores | Critical assessment |
|---|---|---|
| **Intent-gated SASRec:** fuse recent and retrieved historical evidence; train with 32 uniform negatives | 5/8/8/8/4 | Originality: + targeted combination, − established components. Potential: + extensible, − gate may collapse. User: + recent intent and repeated preferences, − cannot verify compatibility. Model: + targets weak separation, − margins may not generalize. Difficulty: + small modules, − causal masking requires care. |
| **Objective-only:** retain SASRec; replace BCE with multi-negative sampled softmax | 2/7/4/9/2 | Originality: + clean adaptation, − standard objective. Potential: + strong baseline, − limited expressiveness. User: + sharper ranking, − no explicit intent mechanism. Model: + more comparisons, − false negatives. Difficulty: + simple, − memory grows. |
| **Transition residual:** add smoothed directed training-transition scores | 4/6/7/5/3 | Originality: + useful integration, − established graphs. Potential: + interpretable, − sparse coverage. User: + plausible continuation, − transitions imply no utility guarantee. Model: + extra evidence, − weak tail support. Difficulty: + sparse counts, − smoothing requires tuning. |

### Selected idea: intent-gated SASRec

Select the first idea for early-stage feasibility. It explicitly targets two user failures: broad associations outranking recent intent, and isolated interactions outweighing repeated narrow preferences. Multi-negative training targets Electronics’ weak positive–negative separation.

At each causal prefix, compute recent mean embedding `r`, historical attention `s = Attention(query=r, keys=prefix_items)`, and gate `g = sigmoid(MLP(r,s))`. Score with `f = LayerNorm(h_SASRec + g*W_r*r + (1-g)*W_s*s)` and shared item embeddings. Optimize sampled softmax against 32 distinct uniform negatives, excluding padding, positives and observed training-history items.

Compare baseline, objective-only and gated variants using unchanged macro validation HR@10. Preserve four-category routing, seeds, `GAGC_DATA_DIR`, trial-local artifacts and the predictor writer. Keep diagnosis methods architecture-compatible; track margins without treating them as validation performance.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable SASRec extension. Proposed improvements remain hypotheses; supplied diagnostics establish neither validation gains nor functional compatibility.

### Insights and directions

**Starting-point insights.** (1) SASRec provides causal sequence features; a residual extension preserves this starting representation. (2) Users report broad associations displacing recent intent; recent-history features could strengthen immediate relevance. (3) Repeated narrow preferences provide stronger evidence than isolated interactions; intent-conditioned historical retrieval could distinguish them without inventing traits.

**Related-work insights.** (1) [Hierarchical long/short-term modeling](https://journal.hep.com.cn/fcs/EN/10.1007/s11704-025-41181-y) motivates retrieving persistent interests using recent intent. [PAN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.15346) supplies closer pooling/gating precedent, reducing originality. (2) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates multiple negatives; Electronics’ 14.95% wrong-ranking rate supports testing separation, without proving generalization. (3) [MQSA-TED](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01056) motivates directional transitions, which cannot establish equipment compatibility.

These define temporal evidence selection, ranking objectives, and directional transitions. Similar embedding-collapse scores across datasets provide little justification for collapse-specific regularization.

### Framework and candidate assessment

Scores: originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty, 0–10; higher difficulty means harder. Benefits (+) and limitations (−) are judgments, not results.

| Idea and mechanism | Scores | Critical assessment |
|---|---|---|
| **Intent-gated SASRec:** recent/older-history fusion; 32 uniform negatives | 4/7/8/8/6 | Originality: + targeted integration, − established pooling/gating. Potential: + extensible, − gate collapse. User: + recency/repeated preferences, − compatibility unknown. Model: + separation target, − margins may not generalize. Difficulty: + small modules, − causal pooling, sampling and deployment integration. |
| **Objective-only:** SASRec with multi-negative CE | 2/7/4/9/3 | Originality: + clean adaptation, − standard. Potential: + strong baseline, − limited expressiveness. User: + sharper ranking, − no intent mechanism. Model: + comparisons, − false negatives. Difficulty: + simple loss, − exclusions/memory. |
| **Transition residual:** smoothed directed training-transition scores | 4/6/7/5/3 | Originality: + useful integration, − established graphs. Potential: + interpretable, − sparse coverage. User: + continuation, − utility unverified. Model: + extra evidence, − weak tail support. Difficulty: + sparse counts, − smoothing/tuning. |

### Selected idea: intent-gated SASRec

Retain the first idea for feasibility. It explicitly targets broad associations outranking recent intent and isolated interactions outweighing repeated narrow preferences; multi-negative training targets Electronics’ weak separation. It does not resolve compatibility or already-satisfied needs.

For each prefix, average the last five embeddings into `r`; attend with `r` over strictly older occurrences to obtain `s`. Preserve repeats. Learn `g=sigmoid(MLP([r,s]))`; use `f=LayerNorm(h+α[g Wr(r)+(1−g) Ws(s)])`. Initialize `α=0` and projections nonzero; mask empty branches. Optimize explicitly uncorrected candidate CE using up to 32 distinct uniform negatives excluding full observed training history and the target.

Use a fixed 2×2 architecture/objective factorial, identical checkpoint rules and equal GPU-second budgets within 43,200 seconds. Compare recent-only, history-only, fixed fusion and overlapping-history pooling only within that budget. Track gate saturation, branch sensitivity and margins. Select solely by unchanged macro validation HR@10; paired-user intervals remain exploratory after selection. Preserve routing, seeds, predictor serialization and diagnosis compatibility. Full implementation specifications follow in the structured fields.
