INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: **Proposal: support-gated directed transition residual for SASRec.** At 0% research progress, prioritize a small, testable extension. Improvements remain hypotheses until measured by pinned macro validation HR@10.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already represents ordered preferences. A residual expert can add recent-transition evidence while preserving this baseline. (2) User feedback identifies broad relevance outranking current intent. Conditioning the residual on recent interactions directly targets that failure. (3) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive rankings, versus 3.03% for Movies_and_TV. This motivates better discrimination, while the similar embedding-collapse scores provide little justification for a collapse-focused intervention.

**Insights from related work.** (1) [TGCL4SR](https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/28789) motivates combining sequence representations with temporal transition evidence. Observed transitions nevertheless cannot certify equipment compatibility. (2) [FamouSRec](https://doi.org/10.1145/3696410.3714663) supports adaptive routing among recommendation experts. A support-dependent gate is a lightweight adaptation, not a reproduced result. (3) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates examining negative-sampling objectives. Better training margins alone do not establish improved held-out ranking.

Three research directions follow: current-intent modeling, evidence-dependent fusion, and ranking supervision. The conceptual framework separates **personal sequence evidence** (SASRec), **population successor evidence** (directed transitions), and **candidate discrimination** (multi-negative objectives). The gap is deciding when population evidence supports an individual recommendation.

Consider three atomic ideas. Scores below are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.

| Idea | Scores | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Support-gated transition residual | 5/8/8/7/4 | O: concrete support routing; P: extensible evidence fusion; U: recent intent and supported next tasks; M: confusing successors; D: small expert | O: established fusion pattern; P: sparse support; U: compatibility remains unknown; M: gains unproven; D: graph bookkeeping |
| Recent/stable preference gate | 4/7/7/6/3 | O: task-specific adaptation; P: reusable intent modeling; U: current and sustained preferences; M: reduces history interference; D: simple pooling | O: familiar architecture; P: limited evidence; U: weak task grounding; M: interference unverified; D: causal masking needed |
| Multi-negative gBCE | 3/7/4/8/2 | O: controlled adaptation; P: strong baseline; U: sharper ranking; M: targets weak margins; D: localized change | O: published objective; P: incremental; U: no explicit task reasoning; M: false negatives; D: additional scoring cost |

Select the transition residual: it explicitly addresses broad associations displacing recent intent and recommendations based on unsupported practical associations, while targeting weak positive–negative separation. Train directed source/target embeddings from training-only adjacent transitions; learn a bounded residual gate using SASRec state and observed transition support. Unsupported sources receive zero residual weight. Population transitions supply behavioral evidence, not proof of compatibility or unsatisfied need.

Ablate sequence-only, fixed fusion, and learned fusion. Preserve four-category routing, seeds, splits, diagnostic interfaces, predictor architecture parity, and the single-GPU budget. Select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10; report Electronics margins as secondary diagnostics.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: **Proposal: evidence-gated directed transition residual for SASRec.** At 0% progress, retain a small extension, but strengthen candidate-specific evidence and bound its contribution. Gains remain unmeasured hypotheses.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already models ordered preferences; an explicit transition branch needs demonstrated complementarity. (2) User feedback identifies broad relevance displacing current intent and unsupported practical associations. Recent, repeatedly observed successors may address both, without establishing compatibility or unmet need. (3) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive rankings versus 3.03% for Movies_and_TV. Similar embedding-similarity scores do not support prioritizing collapse remedies.

**Insights from related work.** (1) [TGCL4SR](https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/28789) motivates transition–sequence fusion; [FPMC](https://archives.iw3c2.org/www2010/pub/pdfs/RendleFreudenthaler2010-FPMC.pdf) and [FOSSIL](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09152) establish substantial precedent for directed factors and preference fusion. (2) [FamouSRec](https://doi.org/10.1145/3696410.3714663) motivates adaptive routing, but source frequency alone cannot justify individual successors. (3) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates objective controls; improved training margins cannot establish validation gains.

Three directions follow: current-intent modeling, evidence-dependent fusion, and ranking supervision. The framework separates **personal sequence evidence**, **population successor evidence**, and **candidate discrimination**. Its central gap is determining when population evidence supports a particular recommendation.

Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.

| Idea | Scores | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Evidence-gated transition residual | 4/7/7/7/6 | O: explicit candidate evidence; P: extensible fusion; U: recent intent and supported successors; M: confusing candidates; D: compact expert | O: extensive precedent; P: sparse edges; U: compatibility unresolved; M: gains unproven; D: sparse lookup and export |
| Recent/stable preference gate | 4/7/7/6/3 | O: task adaptation; P: reusable; U: current and sustained preferences; M: possible interference reduction; D: simple pooling | O: familiar; P: uncertain benefit; U: weak practical grounding; M: interference unverified; D: causal masking |
| Multi-negative gBCE | 3/7/4/8/2 | O: controlled adaptation; P: useful baseline; U: sharper ranking; M: weak-margin target; D: localized change | O: published; P: incremental; U: indirect alignment; M: false negatives; D: scoring cost |

Select the transition residual because it explicitly targets two user failures—broad associations displacing recent intent and unsupported practical associations—and weak positive–negative separation. Count distinct training users per directed edge. Multiply a normalized, bounded transition score by source-support and candidate-edge shrinkage. Unseen edges receive no residual; SASRec retains generalization. This restriction limits only the residual, and cannot guarantee safe or useful recommendations.

Prespecify sequence-only, fixed-fusion, scalar-gate and learned-gate ablations, with auxiliary loss initially disabled. Treat untied/parameter-matched SASRec, FMC/FOSSIL and auxiliary-loss controls as follow-ups within budget. Preserve routing, seeds, diagnostic interfaces and predictor parity. Match GPU-second allocations and validation opportunities; freeze checkpoint rules and tie-breaking before trials. Select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10. Paired user-bootstrap intervals quantify fixed-seed uncertainty, not selection bias ([Cawley–Talbot](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html)); sampled ranking need not transfer to full-catalog ranking ([Krichene–Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/)).
