INFO:self_evolverec:Research reports (2 report(s)):
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: ### Insights and directions

Three starting-point insights guide this proposal. First, SASRec already models causal sequence dependencies; a small residual permits an interpretable intervention. Its benefit must be established against unchanged SASRec. Second, Electronics has 14.95% wrong rankings and a −0.4812 lower-decile margin. These training diagnostics motivate better discrimination but do not establish validation gains. Third, users value recent intent and repeated specific preferences. Broad associations and speculative equipment upgrades reveal insufficient behavioral support, although histories cannot establish compatibility.

Three literature insights suggest complementary directions. [ICSRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14318) transfers supervision between subsequences sharing targets. Shared behavior supplies evidence, rather than verified intent. [SComGNN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.02130) distinguishes relevance from similarity in complementary relationships. Directed historical successors therefore offer a useful signal beyond content resemblance. [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates multiple negatives and calibrated BCE. Objective improvements remain a separate hypothesis from architectural improvements.

### Framework and alternatives

| Direction | Minimal idea | Main uncertainty |
|---|---|---|
| Temporal preference | Gate between short and long causal pooling | Pooling may blur specific intent |
| Behavioral evidence | **Recent-anchor transition lift residual** | Sparse edges and confounding |
| Ranking objective | Replace single-negative BCE with gBCE | False negatives and runtime |

Scores below are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Detailed positive and negative assessments accompany the implementation notes.

| Idea | Scores |
|---|---|
| Two-window pooling | 3/6/7/5/3 |
| Transition lift residual | **4/7/7/6/4** |
| Multi-negative gBCE | 2/7/5/8/4 |

### Selected algorithm

At 0% progress, select the transition lift residual: a small, testable extension of the previous directed-residual inspiration. Replace its learned transition embeddings with a fixed, shrunk population transition statistic, and aggregate evidence across a recent suffix. This isolates whether supported successor evidence helps before adding representation learning.

Count adjacent transitions once per training user and ordered pair. Compute smoothed transition lift relative to target popularity. Add only positive, bounded lift, weighted by edge support and exponentially decaying anchor recency; divide by total anchor weight, including unsupported anchors. Learn one bounded residual amplitude per dataset, initialized near zero.

This explicitly targets two user failures: broad associations outranking recent intent, and specialized upgrades recommended without behavioral support. Repeated population successors provide limited evidence for plausible next actions; they cannot verify ownership, dimensions, compatibility, or satisfied needs. The model-facing target is Electronics’ weak margin tail.

Preserve all four routes, seeds, splits, baseline training settings, and diagnostic interfaces. Compare baseline, last-anchor lift, and recent-suffix lift with matched validation opportunities within the shared budget. Select solely by pinned macro validation HR@10. Report edge coverage, margin changes, and simulator mismatch feedback separately; no improvement is established.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: ### Insights and directions

Three starting-point insights guide this proposal. First, SASRec already models causal sequence dependencies; a small residual permits an interpretable intervention. Its benefit must be established against unchanged SASRec. Second, Electronics has 14.95% wrong rankings and a −0.4812 lower-decile margin. These training diagnostics motivate better discrimination but do not establish validation gains. Third, users value recent intent and repeated specific preferences. Broad associations and speculative upgrades reveal insufficient behavioral support, although histories cannot establish compatibility.

Three literature insights refine the directions. [ICSRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14318) transfers supervision between subsequences sharing targets, but shared behavior is not verified intent. [SComGNN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.02130) distinguishes relevance from similarity. [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates multiple negatives and calibrated BCE; objective changes remain a separate hypothesis. Together these motivate temporal representation, behavioral evidence, and ranking-objective research.

### Framework and alternatives

| Direction | Minimal idea | Main uncertainty |
|---|---|---|
| Temporal preference | Gate short/long causal pooling | Pooling may blur intent |
| Behavioral evidence | **Recent-anchor transition lift residual** | Independent support and confounding |
| Ranking objective | Multi-negative gBCE | False negatives and runtime |

Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Positive and negative assessments appear in implementation notes.

| Idea | Scores |
|---|---|
| Two-window pooling | 3/6/7/5/3 |
| Transition lift residual | **3/6/6/6/5** |
| Multi-negative gBCE | 2/7/5/8/4 |

### Selected algorithm

At 0% progress, retain the transition residual as a controlled hypothesis. It extends the previous inspiration by replacing learned transition embeddings with fixed statistics aggregated over recent anchors. Its closest precedents are [sequential rules](https://github.com/saraLatifi/session-rec) and [FOSSIL](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09152), limiting originality.

Count adjacent edges once per training user. Compute smoothed positive transition lift, shrink by support, aggregate five causal anchors, and learn one bounded amplitude. Crucially, exclude the example’s entire user from training-time edge counts, source totals, target totals, and smoothing priors, following [group isolation](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html). Otherwise every adjacent training target supports itself. Use all training users at inference.

The proposal targets broad associations outranking recent intent and upgrades lacking behavioral support; successors provide limited evidence of plausible next actions. They cannot establish ownership, compatibility, or satisfied needs. Electronics’ weak margin tail supplies the model-facing target. Positive-only evidence cannot suppress unsupported candidates directly.

Preserve all routes, seeds, splits, training settings, and diagnostic interfaces. Compare baseline, last-anchor, and five-anchor variants within 43,200 GPU-seconds. Report support disappearance, frozen-backbone amplitude sensitivity, all checkpoint scores, and actual updates alongside elapsed time. Select solely by pinned macro validation HR@10. [Paired bootstrap](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.bootstrap.html) intervals require available user-level hits and do not correct winner selection. Neither training margins nor [sampled ranking metrics](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/) establish full-catalog gains. No improvement is established.
