tistical confidence interpretation is claimed.\n\nPrespecify two neural runs per category: unchanged baseline BCE and uniform 32-negative sampled CE, with identical encoder, optimizer, training schedule, and baseline checkpoint-selection rule. For CE, use one positive and 32 distinct negatives per valid position; exclude padding, the target, and all known training-history items for that user without consulting held-out labels. Uniform sampling without replacement uses a per-example eligible pool; use all eligible negatives if fewer than 32 exist, and skip a position if none exist. Compute ordinary CE over the positive and sampled negatives without claiming an unbiased full-softmax approximation. This is a fixed objective control, not gBCE or a sampling-corrected estimator.\n\nFor each frozen neural checkpoint, evaluate lambda in {0,0.25,1}, producing six prespecified configurations. If compute limits prevent both objective runs from completing for every category, report the incomplete comparison rather than substituting categories or claiming the factorial experiment was completed. Keep total training, diagnostics, failed trials, and debugging within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Preserve baseline checkpoint rules exactly and log their selection opportunities; fusion selection uses only the unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10. Freeze the grid and trial count before observing results. Cache component scores, user/candidate IDs, checkpoint provenance, and table versions when the evaluator exposes them, without modifying its scoring procedure. Cheap grid evaluation still creates selection opportunities.\n\nRetain the three excluded-fold tables at inference, routing every known user through the persisted fold exclusion. Resolve the exact benchmark user identity from its input record and persisted preprocessing map; never infer identity from sequence content. If the prediction interface does not expose user identity, use a fixed category-specific fold for every request in both validation and deployment and report this variant explicitly; user-fold OOF diagnostic claims then require a separate provenance-bearing dataset and do not describe deployed routing. Previously unseen users use the same fixed category-specific fold. Fix these routing semantics before validation. Do not silently rebuild tables from all users or average folds, because either changes support and score semantics. Serialize the selected model, sparse tables, identities/routing where available, and fusion configuration; update the predictor writer so working/predict.py implements exactly the validated route and scoring.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions, and keep view.model as the actual trained nn.Module. Preserve item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data compatibility. Make log2feats return an explicitly documented feature layout containing neural features and the current prefix's recent item IDs; it must not include future positions. Provide view.predict_score_base. Anonymous train_loader batches cannot establish user ownership. Generate an additional deterministic diagnostic stream directly from view.user_train, retaining category, user ID, prefix endpoint, example ID, and fold; compare residual-on/off scores on identical positive/negative candidates using the appropriate excluded-fold table. Keep anonymous-batch neural diagnostics separate. If provenance or valid paired candidates are unavailable, mark fold-dependent comparisons unavailable. Preserve the distinction between OOF training diagnostics and deployed routing; never recover identity by matching truncated histories.\n\nReport paired corrections and regressions, per-category hit changes, discordant validation hit counts, and macro improvement. If per-user validation hits are available through permitted evaluator outputs, run 10,000 paired bootstrap replicates with RNG seed 42, independently resampling users within each category and averaging category differences equally; report the percentile 95% interval. Otherwise report that this interval is unavailable rather than modifying the evaluator. Winner intervals are exploratory and conditional on frozen models and candidates; they omit training-seed variability, negative-sampling variability, and selection bias. Avoid confirmatory significance claims. The benchmark measures HR@10 against 99 sampled negatives; full-catalog simulator recommendations remain a separate qualitative assessment for the prescribed twenty real training users. User grouping does not establish global chronological causality, and no chronology changes to the pinned splits are proposed.\n\nDefer learned gating, Bayesian posterior thresholds, Markov-probability fusion, sequential rules, and V-SKNN to separately budgeted work. They are useful alternatives, but adding them to this early-stage selection grid would increase compute and validation-selection exposure. Bayesian uncertainty would require handling estimated-background uncertainty and repeated-user dependence rather than treating a Beta approximation as calibrated evidence.",
