{
  "description": "Freeze each category’s SASRec and add a candidate-independent, scale-normalized cosine residual from its most recent K interaction events. Include the unchanged baseline in validation selection. Retain the original experiment and add prespecified controls for recency, popularity, repeated events, numerical stability and export consistency.",
  "motivation": "The diagnosis suggests that broad relevance sometimes overwhelms recent specific interests. A small residual tests whether emphasizing those interests improves the fixed benchmark, while acknowledging that similarity can amplify substitutes and cannot establish compatibility, fulfilled needs or current purchase intent. Shared SASRec embeddings may make the added signal redundant. At 10% research progress, this controlled experiment remains preferable to introducing a coherence gate or brittle text-based penalties before establishing incremental value.",
  "implementation_notes": "Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four category routes, original input splits, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment default of 200. Train the baseline once per category within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget, recording its existing checkpoint-selection rule and every inspected validation result. Freeze parameters and use evaluation mode. No test labels, hidden files, external data, pretrained weights or evaluator changes are permitted.\n\nUse one shared K and alpha configuration across categories, selected by pinned macro validation HR@10; break ties in favor of alpha=0. The primary search remains K={3,10}, alpha={0.05,0.10,0.20}, plus baseline. Prespecify supplementary controls before inspecting their results: last-item, full available causal prefix, uniformly sampled K events without replacement and oldest-K, using the same alpha grid. Use the same sampled event subsets across candidates and alpha values. A training-count popularity control uses log1p(item training count), with its own training-margin scale normalization. Record any additional K=5, robust-scale or unique-item pooling experiment as an expansion of the search scope. Preserve repeated events in the main representation. Event-weighted versus unique-item pooling is an ablation, not a preprocessing change. Shuffling events within the same mean-pooled window cannot test order sensitivity.\n\nEstimate category- and K-specific scales from the first ten batches of a separate nonshuffled loader over the existing causal training examples, using a dedicated generator seeded 42 for any negative draws. Reuse these examples and negatives across residual variants; record their IDs or an equivalent reproducible specification. Exclude positions with padding and nonfinite margins. Compute population standard deviations in float64. Set k=0 when fewer than two valid pairs exist, either standard deviation is nonfinite, or the cosine-margin standard deviation is at most 1e-3; otherwise set k=min(std(base_margin)/std(cosine_margin),100). These fixed numerical safeguards are engineering choices, not validated guarantees. Normalize embeddings and history means with epsilon=1e-8, leaving padding and empty histories zero. Scale normalization is not probability calibration: it ignores margin means and correctness. Record margin correlation because Var(base_margin + alpha*k*cosine_margin)=sigma_base^2*(1+alpha^2+2*alpha*rho) when the ratio is uncapped and nondegenerate. If robust alternatives are explored, specify normalized MAD or IQR and zero-scale fallbacks in advance; they introduce additional configurations.\n\nSave checkpoint, K, alpha, category-specific scale, normalization epsilon, dtype, scale estimator, safeguards, sampling seeds, and event-pooling policy beneath the trial directory. Update the predictor writer to reconstruct identical scoring and category routing. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device) and run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions. view.model must remain the actual deployed nn.Module. The compatibility view can return concatenated SASRec and recent-history features of shape B×L×2D; predict_score must support that layout and expose predict_score_base. Preserve item_emb, user_train, meta_data and review_data, explicitly marking unavailable fields and simulator traits unavailable. Only public histories, titles, descriptions and timestamps may support additional analyses; ignore the generic metadata examples containing prohibited fields.\n\nVerify diagnostic and exported scores in evaluation mode on identical prefixes, candidate IDs and frozen weights. Use short left-padded histories, repeated items, empty prefixes where supported, and modified future tokens; earlier-position scores must remain invariant. Check finite values and float32 score agreement with atol=1e-5 and rtol=1e-5; compare rankings with explicit handling of numerical ties. A mismatch invalidates the ablation until corrected.\n\nReport baseline and every configuration’s per-category and macro validation HR@10, paired user hit differences, and counts gained/lost. For descriptive uncertainty, perform 10,000 paired bootstrap replicates with seed 42, resampling 200 users within each category and averaging category differences equally. These intervals condition on trained models, fixed candidates and categories; they exclude seed and candidate-draw variation and are not selection-adjusted when applied to the chosen winner. Six prespecified baseline comparisons may receive Holm-adjusted paired tests or simultaneous intervals, but these do not remove winner optimism or cover later adaptive searches. Call validation results exploratory. Training-only chronological inner windows could support a future tuning protocol with cutoff-specific preprocessing and auxiliary retraining, but incur budget costs and cannot retroactively make inspected validation independent.\n\nInspect target-popularity buckets defined from training counts, cosine-residual/popularity correlation, and repeat-target versus unseen-target slices wherever permitted targets and histories support them. Keep these analyses separate from selection. Full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for twenty real training users, five per category, cannot directly benefit from recommending consumed items. Their qualitative feedback does not establish benchmark gains or unobserved preferences. No executable replacement train.py is specified because the supplied input contains no baseline implementation or predictor interface.",
  "pseudocode": "Prespecify primary configurations, controls, checkpoint rule, numerical settings and complete search scope.\nFor each of the four categories:\n  Train SASRec through the existing pipeline; record checkpoint selection; freeze parameters; enter eval mode.\n  E[i] = normalize(item_embedding[i], eps=1e-8); E[0] = 0.\n  Reconstruct fixed causal calibration pairs from training only with dedicated seed 42.\n  For K in {3,10}:\n    For each input position t:\n      events = nonpadding inputs through t, including input[t], excluding target[t].\n      r[t] = normalize(mean(E[last min(K,len(events)) events]), eps=1e-8).\n      Use zero for an empty prefix; preserve repeated events.\n    On identical valid calibration positions and negatives:\n      db = base_score(t,positive) - base_score(t,negative).\n      dc = dot(r[t],E[positive]) - dot(r[t],E[negative]).\n    Compute float64 population standard deviations sb and sc over finite pairs.\n    k = 0 if insufficient pairs, nonfinite scales, or sc <= 1e-3; else min(sb/sc,100).\n    Record scales, margin means, correlation and sampling specification.\n    score(t,i;alpha,K) = base_score(t,i) + alpha*k*dot(r[t],E[i]).\nCompare baseline and six shared (K,alpha) configurations, alpha in {0.05,0.10,0.20}, on identical pinned validation users and candidates.\nSelect highest equally weighted four-category macro HR@10; prefer baseline on ties, then smaller alpha and K.\nRun prespecified last-item, full-prefix, random-K, oldest-K and training-popularity controls with the same alpha grid and normalization protocol; disclose whether they are diagnostic only or included in selection before examining results.\nSerialize all selected settings and write the matching predictor.\nVerify exported/diagnostic score agreement, padding, repeated-event behavior, finite scores and future-token invariance.\nRun residual-on/off diagnostics on identical training pairs; report corrected/regressed pairs separately from validation.\nReport all configurations and paired category gains/losses; bootstrap users jointly across models within categories for descriptive macro intervals.\nInspect training-count popularity and repeat/unseen slices without treating them as evidence of unobserved user needs.",
