INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: The best early-stage direction is a **bounded, recent-source transition residual**, preserving SASRec when evidence is sparse. Its benefits remain hypotheses until measured on the pinned validation benchmark.

**Insights and directions.** Three starting-point insights motivate evidence control: (1) Directed successor relationships can distinguish specific continuations from broad similarity. Separate source/target embeddings express this asymmetry. (2) Distinct-user support can reduce reliance on isolated transitions. Counts measure repeated observations, not functional compatibility. (3) Recent sources can reduce the influence of older interests. A bounded residual limits disruption to SASRec’s generalization.

Three literature insights suggest complementary directions: (1) [ICSRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14318) supports learning intent structure from histories. Its subsequence alignment does not establish actual purchase purposes. (2) [Directed graph projections](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09209) motivate asymmetric relationship modeling. Observed transitions still cannot certify complementarity. (3) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates testing negative-sampling objectives. Electronics’ 13.08% training wrong-ranking rate makes separation worth investigating, without establishing validation gains.

**Framework.** Organize methods along three axes: context selection (full history versus recent suffix), relationship evidence (latent similarity versus supported directed transitions), and supervision (single versus multiple negatives). SASRec supplies full-history generalization; the proposed residual adds recent, supported relationships; objective changes provide an independent control.

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are 0–10; difficulty increases with score. Each cell gives a positive and negative reason.

| Idea | Originality | Potential | User alignment | Model alignment | Difficulty |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| A: Recent, support-gated directed residual | 5: specific combination / established components | 7: extensible evidence control / sparse coverage | 8: specific recent continuations / no compatibility guarantee | 7: candidate-specific separation / gains uncertain | 4: small branch / sparse lookup integration |
| B: Uniform multi-negative gBCE | 3: established objective / little novelty | 7: reusable baseline / compute cost | 4: sharper ranking / no explicit current-intent mechanism | 8: targets weak margins / training metrics insufficient | 3: local loss change / calibration care |
| C: Scratch-trained title successor scorer | 5: directional text sharing / familiar approach | 6: supports sparse edges / weak text coverage | 6: finer associations / similarity can mislead | 5: shares evidence / may add noise | 6: available inputs / vocabulary and encoder work |

**Select A at 10% progress.** It explicitly targets two observed user failures: broad associations without specific continuation evidence, and older interests dominating current direction. It targets the model’s weak positive–negative separation through candidate-specific scores. It does not claim to identify satisfied needs.

For recent sources, sum decayed, bounded asymmetric scores weighted by distinct-user source and edge support; cap the total correction. Initialize its amplitude near zero and train jointly. Compare SASRec, A, and objective-only B under matched budgets; select exclusively by four-category macro validation HR@10. Preserve routing, splits, seeds, and the predictor writer. Report support coverage and backbone/residual margins; embedding alignment alone is not evidence of collapse.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: The best early-stage direction remains a **bounded, recent-source transition residual**, with stricter provenance and evidence controls. Benefits remain hypotheses until measured on the pinned validation benchmark.

**Insights and directions.** Three starting-point insights motivate evidence control: (1) Directed successors can distinguish specific continuations from broad similarity, but transitions cannot certify compatibility. (2) Distinct-user support reduces reliance on isolated observations, provided training labels cannot supply their own support. (3) Recent sources can reduce older-interest influence; bounding the correction protects SASRec when evidence is weak.

Three literature insights sharpen these directions: (1) [ICSRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14318) supports history-derived intent learning without establishing purchase purpose. (2) [FPMC](https://www.ra.ethz.ch/cdstore/www2010/www/p811.pdf) already adds asymmetric transitions to personalization, limiting originality claims. Directed graph projections likewise motivate asymmetry, not proven complementarity. (3) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates objective controls. Electronics’ 13.08% training wrong-ranking rate warrants investigation but predicts no validation gain.

**Framework.** Three axes organize comparisons: context (full history/recent suffix), relationship evidence (latent/count-supported directed), and supervision (single/multiple negatives). SASRec supplies generalization; the residual adds supported pairwise evidence. Aggregating three recent sources remains first-order pairwise scoring, not third-order modeling.

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each cell provides a positive and negative reason.

| Idea | Originality | Potential | User alignment | Model alignment | Difficulty |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| A: Recent, support-gated directed residual | 3: specific gate / FPMC precedent | 6: interpretable control / sparse coverage | 8: recent specific continuations / no compatibility guarantee | 7: candidate separation / uncertain gains | 6: small branch / provenance and lookup complexity |
| B: Uniform multi-negative gBCE | 3: principled control / established | 7: reusable baseline / compute cost | 4: sharper ranking / no intent mechanism | 8: weak-margin target / training metrics insufficient | 3: local change / calibration care |
| C: Scratch-trained title successor scorer | 5: directional sharing / familiar components | 6: sparse-edge sharing / weak text coverage | 6: finer associations / misleading similarity | 5: shared evidence / noise | 6: permitted inputs / encoder work |

**Select A at 10% progress.** It explicitly targets broad associations lacking specific continuation evidence and older interests dominating current direction. Candidate-specific corrections target weak positive–negative separation. It cannot identify satisfied needs.

Use the latest three sources, normalized recency weights, separate source/target embeddings, distinct-user support, and a bounded correction initialized near zero. Build counts only from training histories; subtract each training user’s entire contribution using explicit upstream user IDs. Never infer identity from padded sequences. Singleton evidence is disabled consistently; remaining count-distribution mismatch must still be measured. Save the exact inference tables and scorer.

Prioritize baseline, count-only augmentation, and learned augmentation under matched budgets; test ungated, symmetric, and objective-only controls if affordable. Select exclusively by four-category macro validation HR@10. Preserve routing, seeds, splits, predictor parity, and diagnosis contracts. Report support coverage, backbone/residual margins, category deltas, and paired-user uncertainty. One changed hit equals 0.125 macro percentage points. Bootstrap intervals exclude training randomness and selection optimism; sampled HR@10 does not establish full-catalog quality.
