{
  "description": "Add an evidence-gated causal temporal representation residual to each dataset-specific SASRec. Form recent-five and full-available-prefix means of unit item embeddings. For each branch, calculate support from a strictly preceding pool, excluding the current item. A fixed attenuation combines preceding-history count, directional concentration and agreement with the current item. A two-way learned gate mixes the attenuated, smoothly bounded pools before an additive residual changes the SASRec state. This revises the original normalization and self-inclusive agreement while retaining tensor-valued features and candidate dot-product scoring.",
  "motivation": "The mechanism explicitly targets two user-observed failures: older broad interests overriding current intent, and isolated interactions receiving more preference weight than their evidence warrants. Recent pooling provides a current-context alternative; prefix pooling provides sustained context; preceding-history support prevents the current item from being its own supporting observation. Unsupported actions still influence the unchanged SASRec pathway, so this intervention limits additional extrapolation rather than guaranteeing denoising. It cannot establish equipment ownership, compatibility, replacement needs, or whether a purchase satisfied a need. The model-facing target remains weak positive–negative separation, especially Electronics’ 14.95% wrong-ranking rate and negative margin p10. These training diagnostics motivate an experiment, not an expected validation improvement. At 0% progress this remains a feasible primary hypothesis; multi-negative gBCE is a strong independent follow-up, but lacks explicit mechanisms for the two required user failures.",
  "implementation_notes": "Use histories only for the first experiment. Do not use unavailable ratings, reviews, categories, prices, stores, simulator traits, external datasets, or pretrained weights. Supplied metadata examples do not authorize unavailable fields. Titles, descriptions and timestamps remain permitted but unused.\n\nExact causal construction: Let e_t be the backbone item-table embedding before positional embeddings, scaling and dropout. In float32, define x_t=e_t/sqrt(sum(e_t^2)+1e-4) for nonpadding items and zero otherwise. This smooth normalization bounds each vector norm by one without a hard normalization boundary. The constant 1e-4 is fixed before trials. Do not detach embeddings or support features; gradients flow through the complete branch. Smooth normalization reduces the specific near-zero gradient issue of hard L2 normalization but does not guarantee stable optimization.\n\nDefine R_t as the arithmetic mean of x over the latest five nonpadding interactions through t, and P_t over every nonpadding interaction through t available after baseline truncation. Define Rminus_t over the latest five strictly preceding nonpadding interactions and Pminus_t over every available strictly preceding interaction. These preceding windows exclude t; Rminus_t may therefore include one older interaction absent from R_t. Use prefix sums and counts, or an equivalent gather implementation. Do not silently implement five positions when internal padding could occur: windows are defined over nonpadding interactions. Empty means are zero. No pooling accesses interactions beyond the prediction position or outside the supplied sequence.\n\nFor either preceding branch mean m and its count n, calculate R=norm(m) and agreement A=clip(dot(x_t,m)/sqrt(sum(m^2)+0.01^2),0,1). Define rho=1[n>=2] * n/(n+2) * R * A. Padding positions have rho=0. The count is an observation count, not independent-user evidence; repeated item occurrences count separately. With equal weights, effective sample size equals n. At most five observations enter the recent support branch, while the prefix branch uses its full available count. R measures directional concentration of approximately unit vectors. It is not calibrated uncertainty; cancellation can represent multiple legitimate interests, and a coherent broad history can support an irrelevant action. The n>=2 rule is an explicit engineering prior, not a learned or validated threshold. It makes both residual branches exactly zero when fewer than two preceding interactions exist and prevents singleton self-agreement. It does not establish statistical independence between learned embeddings and their training targets.\n\nBound each inclusive pool with q(m)=m/(norm(m)+0.1). Its norm is below one; its limiting Jacobian at zero is bounded by 10. Use float32 pooling and denominator arithmetic even if the backbone later uses mixed precision. This preserves magnitude information and avoids amplifying every nonzero pool to a full-strength direction. Set z_t=concat(h_t, rho_recent, rho_prefix, log1p(n_recent), log1p(n_prefix)). Compute g_t=softmax(W*z_t+b), v_t=g_t[0]*rho_recent*q(R_t)+g_t[1]*rho_prefix*q(P_t), and f_t=(h_t+a*v_t)*valid_t. Set a=2*sigmoid(theta), initialized to 0.01 with theta=logit(0.005). Initialize W and b to zero. Fixed fusion uses g=(0.5,0.5), retaining the same support functions and learned amplitude. Baseline bypasses the entire residual. The representation correction has norm at most two; candidate-logit changes are bounded only relative to candidate embedding norms. Small initial amplitude can slow adaptation, so log its value, gradient and actual score contribution rather than assuming useful learning.\n\nInclude theta in an optimizer group without weight decay; preserve existing backbone parameter groups and apply the baseline policy to W and b. Preserve the baseline architecture, loss, optimizer settings, negative sampler distribution, training examples and preprocessing. Support attenuation is part of this revised idea; fixed-versus-adaptive fusion isolates learned mixing conditional on that attenuation, not the attenuation itself.\n\nPrimary comparison: unchanged SASRec, evidence-gated fixed fusion, and evidence-gated adaptive fusion. Allocate at most 12,000 GPU-seconds per variant across four categories, equally divided, reserving 7,200 seconds for validation, simulator scoring, diagnostics, parity checks, debugging and failed trials. Each category stops at its configured SASREC_EPOCHS limit, normally 200, or its 3,000-second training cap. Do not impose a separate 200-epoch ceiling that overrides an explicit environment setting. Use one terminal complete four-category bundle per variant for primary selection; do not select intermediate checkpoints. Checkpoint training progress for recovery without evaluating additional selection candidates. Log milestone overshoot and charge it to the reserve without exceeding the shared 43,200-second total. Synchronize CUDA at timing boundaries and conservatively charge occupied GPU wall time when phases cannot be separated. Sum consumption across concurrent trial slots; concurrency does not multiply the budget. Revise an impractical schedule once before comparative execution and validation inspection, recording the reason.\n\nPreprocessing seed remains 0 and training seed remains 42. For compatible variants, create and save one baseline initialization per category, then restore exactly those backbone weights rather than relying on seed reuse after adding modules. Use separate RNG streams for module initialization, sequence sampling/shuffling, training negatives, loader workers and dropout where the harness permits. Preserve the baseline seed-42 sample stream and seed workers explicitly. Added gates have deterministic zero initialization and introduce no additional dropout. Verify matched sample and negative streams on comparable initial updates. If exact stream isolation is unavailable, document the mismatch instead of claiming paired optimization. Record software, hardware, optimizer updates, examples, valid positions, effective epochs, throughput and charged GPU time. Equal elapsed time can imply different update counts; fixed-seed comparisons do not establish robustness across seeds.\n\nPreserve DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four dataset routes, and int(os.environ.get('SASREC_EPOCHS','200')). Save architecture configuration, all numerical constants, parameters, mappings, initializations and logs beneath the trial directory. Keep execution single-GPU. Generate working/predict.py using the identical model implementation and saved configuration, including branch mode and support rules. Inference requires no metadata or sparse transition tables. Do not change the host evaluator, input splits, masking rules or candidate generation.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions, with public batches (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor B x L. view.model references the complete trained module and view.item_emb the backbone item table. log2feats returns the augmented B x L x D tensor; predict_score remains the original candidate dot product with explicitly correct broadcasting. Preserve user_train, meta_data and review_data interfaces without filling unavailable fields. Compute original margin diagnostics through the complete scorer. Add named diagnostics for branch weights, support, amplitude, residual norms and backbone-versus-augmented margins on identical batches. Define each aggregation and number of valid positions. Embedding similarity describes the backbone table, not the complete scorer, and does not establish recommendation diversity or severe collapse. Category/review analyses must be marked unavailable; empty category lists do not support substantive conclusions.\n\nBefore training comparisons, verify causal prefix invariance in evaluation mode, left padding, internal-padding handling, empty and short histories, truncation, exactly zero residual with insufficient preceding evidence, bounded residual norms, finite gradients near zero pools, checkpoint reloads and all category routes. Verify that changing a future sequence suffix cannot change earlier branch outputs. Test the preceding-window construction explicitly to prevent off-by-one self-inclusion. Compare training-inference versus exported float32 scores and candidate versus chunked full-catalog scores with atol=1e-5 and rtol=1e-4, recording maximum errors and ranking disagreements. Cover candidate broadcasting, final valid position, seen-item masks, ties, batching and catalog chunk sizes. Resolve top-10 disagreements before accepting the export. Use the host’s unchanged validation protocol.\n\nAdditional controls are valuable but are not extra primary candidates in this allocation: recent-only pooling, prefix-only pooling, adaptive fusion without support attenuation, the original normalized residual, and an ungated pooled-history candidate-dot-product term motivated by FOSSIL. The latter can identify a similarity shortcut; fixed-versus-adaptive fusion alone cannot. Declare a separate budget allocation before training these controls. On existing checkpoints, no-retraining branch overrides may be reported as diagnostics if reserve permits, but they are not substitutes for independently trained ablations and cannot enter primary selection.\n\nBefore claiming suppression of isolated interactions, conduct training-only synthetic input perturbations on fixed checkpoints, using an analysis RNG seed 42. Select eligible training prefixes with at least five interactions using a recorded deterministic sample. Replace one selected recent interaction with a uniformly sampled nonpadding catalog item absent from that prefix; keep sequence length, candidates and the remaining prefix fixed. Also remove that same position and repad to baseline length. Cache the exact prefixes, positions, replacements and permissible candidate lists. Report paired score changes, top-10 overlap and residual-support changes for baseline, fixed and adaptive models. Replacement/removal is a sensitivity diagnostic: altered histories have no known counterfactual target, may contain valid new interests, and cannot establish improved accuracy or denoising. Separately stratify unperturbed support and margins by history length and branch support; report zero-support coverage and whether the intervention disproportionately suppresses short histories.\n\nRun the required twenty-real-training-user full-catalog unseen-item simulator protocol, five users per category. Record clear matches, uncertain relevance, unsupported preference extrapolation and recent-intent matches separately from equipment assumptions and satisfied needs. Do not infer unobserved diversity or conformity traits. Positive simulator feedback and improved training margins do not establish sampled validation or full-catalog generalization.\n\nSelect solely by the pinned four-category macro validation HR@10 using 200 users/category, 99 unique negative candidates and evaluation seed 42. Exact ties prefer baseline, then fixed fusion, then adaptive fusion. Retain every attempted variant, failure, aggregate score, exposed category score and compute cost. Historical inspirations already represent adaptive search; the current primary comparison does not erase that history. Repeated validation selection creates optimism even with four-category averaging. Label selected gains exploratory and freeze the final predictor before the host’s separate held-out evaluation; never inspect hidden test files or metrics.\n\nIf the unchanged host exposes per-user hit indicators, bootstrap paired user differences independently within each category, using the same resampled indices for both models and averaging the four category differences equally. Use 10,000 CPU replicates, analysis RNG seed 42, and percentile 95% intervals; report category deltas and discordant hit counts. One hit changes category HR by 0.005 and macro HR by 0.00125. Intervals condition on fixed candidates and trained models, omit training-seed and candidate variability, and do not correct winner selection. If hits are unavailable, omit intervals. Sampled HR@10 measures the pinned candidate task; do not infer full-catalog ordering from it. No experiments, improved metrics, calibrated confidence, or causal user-intent identification are claimed.",
  "pseudocode": "DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']\nepoch_limit = int(os.environ.get('SASREC_EPOCHS', '200'))\n\n# Primary modes: baseline, fixed fusion, adaptive fusion.\n# Preserve the seed-0 preprocessing and seed-42 baseline training streams.\nfor category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n    restore_identical_saved_backbone_initialization(category)\n    model = DatasetSASRecWithEvidenceGatedFusion(saved_config)\n    # W=b=0; theta=logit(0.005); all constants serialized.\n\n    def bounded_pool(m):\n        return m / (norm(m, dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 0.1)\n\n    def preceding_support(current, preceding_mean, preceding_count):\n        concentration = norm(preceding_mean, dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        denominator = sqrt(sum(preceding_mean**2, dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 0.01**2)\n        agreement = clamp(dot(current, preceding_mean) / denominator, 0, 1)\n        n = preceding_count[..., None].float()\n        return (n >= 2) * n / (n + 2) * concentration * agreement\n\n    def log2feats(sequence):\n        valid = sequence != 0\n        h = backbone.log2feats(sequence)\n        if mode == 'baseline':\n            return h\n        e = item_emb(sequence).float()\n        x = e / sqrt(sum(e**2, dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 1e-4)\n        x = x * valid[..., None]\n\n        recent, _ = causal_nonpadding_mean(x, valid, window=5, include_current=True)\n        prefix, _ = causal_nonpadding_mean(x, valid, window='all', include_current=True)\n        previous_recent, nr = causal_nonpadding_mean(x, valid, window=5, include_current=False)\n        previous_prefix, np = causal_nonpadding_mean(x, valid, window='all', include_current=False)\n        rr = preceding_support(x, previous_recent, nr)\n        rp = preceding_support(x, previous_prefix, np)\n\n        if mode == 'fixed':\n            weights = broadcast([0.5, 0.5], h)\n        else:\n            gate_input = concat(h.float(), rr, rp, log1p(nr)[..., None], log1p(np)[..., None])\n            weights = softmax(linear(gate_input), dim=-1)\n        residual = weights[..., 0:1] * rr * bounded_pool(recent)\n        residual += weights[..., 1:2] * rp * bounded_pool(prefix)\n        amplitude = 2 * sigmoid(theta)  # initialized to 0.01\n        return (h + (amplitude * residual).to(h.dtype)) * valid[..., None]\n\n    def predict_score(features, items):\n        return baseline_broadcasted_dot(features, item_emb(items))\n\n    for _, sequence, positive, negative in baseline_train_loader:\n        features = log2feats(sequence)\n        pos = predict_score(features, positive)\n        neg = predict_score(features, negative)\n        loss = unchanged_baseline_loss(pos, neg, baseline_valid_mask)\n        optimize_with_baseline_groups_and_zero_decay_theta(loss)\n        enforce_configured_epoch_and_charged_compute_caps()\n\n    save_complete_model_mapping_constants_mode_and_rng_states_under_trial_directory()\n    write_predictor_with_identical_model_and_scoring()\n    verify_causality_preceding_windows_gradients_reload_and_score_parity()\n    run_required_diagnostics_simulator_and_prespecified_sensitivity_checks()\n\n# One terminal four-category checkpoint bundle per primary variant.\n# Select solely by unchanged pinned macro validation HR@10.\n# Exact ties: baseline, fixed fusion, adaptive fusion.\n# All search GPU work and failures count toward 43,200 GPU-seconds.\n# Optional paired bootstrap uses only host-exposed per-user validation hits.\n# Freeze the selected predictor before independent held-out evaluation.",
