  "description": "Train baseline SASRec with mild, unscaled whole-item content dropout: zero selected nonpadding input lookup activations before positional addition and any residual branch carrying the original item content. Preserve original attention and padding masks, positions, targets, and inference scoring. Disable augmentation at inference.",
  "motivation": "The supplied experiments establish uneven sampled training separation, with Electronics weakest, but provide no validation evidence favoring additional recent-history branches. Missing-content augmentation offers a distinct, inexpensive hypothesis. Related work motivates investigating robustness but does not directly validate this operator: interaction deletion, learned-token masking, and zeroing lookup activations have different semantics. The intervention may remove narrow preference evidence or encourage popularity shortcuts, so clean host validation accuracy remains decisive.",
  "implementation_notes": "Integrate the provided function immediately after the baseline item lookup and its existing sqrt(hidden_dimension) scaling, before positional addition, ordinary embedding dropout, and any residual branch that would otherwise retain unmasked item content. Multiplying by sqrt(d) before versus after unscaled zeroing is algebraically equivalent; preserve the baseline operation order. Compute padding, attention, and target/loss masks from original IDs. Preserve original positional indexing, truncation, padding-zeroing operations, causal attention, final-feature extraction, and scoring. Never replace sequence IDs or targets with padding IDs, mutate item_emb.weight, or mask candidate/output embeddings. With tied embeddings, output-side gradients can still update items whose input activations were dropped. An erased slot remains attention-eligible and can acquire nonzero states through positional embeddings, LayerNorm bias, attention, and residual transformations; call this missing item content rather than complete interaction removal.\n\nUse unscaled zeroing as the primary operator, preserving the original proposal. Do not silently replace it with PyTorch-style inverted dropout: dividing surviving item vectors by 1-p changes their scale relative to positional embeddings during training. LayerNorm does not generally remove that change because only the item component is rescaled. An inverted variant would be a separately named, budgeted experiment. Accept p in [0,1), with p=0 an exact no-op. Keep the earliest nonpadding interaction in each sequence unmasked; singleton histories therefore receive no augmentation. For all other valid positions, draw independent Bernoulli masks with p=0.1. This anchor choice is a design assumption, not an empirically established optimum. Do not add consistency loss, multiple-positive supervision, or changed negative sampling in the primary comparison.\n\nCreate one persistent device-compatible torch.Generator per single-GPU training process, seeded explicitly with 42 for augmentation. Pass it to every augmentation draw; never recreate it per batch. Separate it from default CPU/CUDA RNG streams so extra draws do not shift ordinary-dropout randomness when execution order and tensor shapes match. Match initial model weights and isolate minibatch ordering and negative sampling as well. Save augmentation generator state, Python/NumPy/default CPU and CUDA RNG states, sampler/shuffler states, optimizer, scheduler, completed step/epoch, and configuration for resumption. Restore generator state on its compatible device. Record software versions and deterministic-kernel settings; isolated generators do not guarantee cross-platform determinism. The generator itself is not a parameter or automatically included in model.state_dict().\n\nRetain DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, CDs_and_Vinyl, dataset routing, existing splits, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and SASREC_EPOCHS environment behavior with default 200. Train each trial on one GPU. Preserve baseline loss, regularization, optimizer, sampler, checkpoint schedule, and checkpoint-selection rules. Save content_dropout_p, operator='unscaled_zero_content', anchor='earliest_nonpadding', and augmentation RNG configuration in checkpoint metadata. Update the predictor writer to reconstruct the same model configuration and explicitly call eval(); generated working/predict.py must never augment inputs or consume augmentation RNG state during inference. Write all artifacts beneath the trial directory.\n\nPredeclare only baseline p=0 and augmentation p=0.1, subject to the actual remaining shared budget. Record all attempted configurations and actual GPU seconds, including training, diagnosis, debugging, and failures; do not assume the full 43,200 GPU-seconds remain after earlier attempts. Give the two conditions equal predeclared compute allocations, preserving the existing checkpoint-selection rule. Compare common completed training steps and report completed epochs and runtime alongside host validation results. Additional probabilities, clean/corrupted training mixtures, or inverted scaling must replace planned work or use recorded remaining budget, rather than expand search silently. Current Bernoulli masking already produces a stochastic mixture of affected and unaffected positions; it does not guarantee a chosen fraction of wholly clean sequences.\n\nSelect using only the unchanged host evaluator: 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negative candidates, evaluation seed 42, and equal-weight four-category macro HR@10. Report category HR@10 values when exposed. Do not alter the host evaluator or use auxiliary corruption metrics for checkpoint/configuration selection. Access no hidden tests, external datasets, or pretrained weights. Twenty training users' full-catalog unseen-item recommendations remain exploratory and separate from benchmark selection. Missing equipment, compatibility, ownership, satisfaction, ratings, item categories, and simulator traits remain unknown.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning {'metrics': metrics, 'metric_definitions': metric_definitions}. view.model is the actual trained module, and item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data retain their compatibility contracts. Preserve existing clean loader-negative margins and embedding-alignment probes; label these descriptive training diagnostics. Undefined statistics are null, not zero, and unavailable category breakdowns remain empty. Return gradient finiteness as null if no gradients are retained. Save each submodule's prior training flag, run clean diagnosis with eval() and no_grad(), and restore individual flags in finally. Optional perturbations should use a functional embedding override without activating ordinary dropout, changing weights, or consuming training RNG streams; restore any touched buffers, hooks, and RNG states in finally.\n\nFor a small, predeclared auxiliary audit, use fixed eligible training-user histories and corruption counts m in {1,2}. At each count, require at least 2m+1 usable interactions; retain the final observed interaction and compare masking the m most recent preceding items, the m earliest items, and m uniformly sampled earlier positions. Run ten deterministic shared draws with audit seed 42; deterministic cases simply repeat unchanged. Separately mask the final item's content once, retaining its original position and extracting the same final sequence feature. Never compact sequences. Freeze original-history exclusions and each user's candidates across models and corruptions. Report eligible-user counts and top-10 overlap, rank displacement, recommendation popularity, and cross-user similarity. These are acute interventions, not retraining results or causal estimates.\n\nReport target ranks and absolute clean/corrupted HR@10 or NDCG@10 only where the authorized interface exposes targets and candidate scoring. For validation perturbation audits, proceed only if such an auxiliary interface exists without modifying the pinned evaluator; otherwise omit them and disclose the limitation. Use identical users, targets, candidates, and corruption draws across models. A smaller drop does not establish robustness if clean accuracy is worse. Where per-user outcomes exist, average draws within user, then jointly bootstrap users within category across conditions with 2,000 replicates and bootstrap seed 42; equally average the four category effects. These intervals condition on fixed models/candidates and exclude training-seed and adaptive-selection uncertainty.\n\nDefine auxiliary popularity strata from training interaction counts only, using frozen catalog item-count tertiles and reporting ties, boundaries, and group sizes; stratify usable history length as 1-5, 6-10, and 11+. Sparse groups warrant descriptive reporting rather than strong conclusions. Compare training-frequency popularity scores as a history-insensitive reference. If budget permits, prioritize shuffled-order and within-category length/popularity-matched swapped-history controls; describe matching rules before running and exclude unmatched users. A trained last-item-only model is a separate budgeted control, whereas masking a full model to retain only the last item is an acute, potentially out-of-distribution intervention. Shuffling measures order sensitivity, not contextual usefulness by itself. Keep popularity-matched candidate audits and training-user full-catalog audits separate from pinned selection.\n\nCheck exact evaluation-mode parity between p=0 and p=0.1 with matched weights; augmentation-disabled paths must return the original activations without drawing randomness. Verify whole-vector masking, earliest-item preservation, singleton/empty histories, original mask preservation, future-token invariance, finite gradients, untouched embedding weights, output-side tied-embedding gradients, and exact augmentation-mask continuation after checkpoint/resume. Verify candidate-score parity after loading generated predict.py for all four routes, using atol=1e-6 and rtol=1e-5 and investigating ranking discrepancies. No experiments or performance measurements have been executed in this response.",
  "pseudocode": "import torch\n\n\ndef item_content_dropout(sequence, item_vectors, p=0.1,\n                         training=False, generator=None):\n    \"\"\"sequence [B,L]; scaled lookup activations [B,L,D].\n\n    Unscaled zero-content corruption. Original IDs determine all masks.\n    The caller owns a persistent generator compatible with this device.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not 0.0 <= p < 1.0:\n        raise ValueError('p must be in [0, 1)')\n    if not training or p == 0.0:\n        return item_vectors  # Exact no-op; consume no augmentation RNG.\n    if generator is None:\n        raise ValueError('training augmentation requires a dedicated generator')\n\n    valid = sequence.ne(0)\n    first_valid = valid & valid.long().cumsum(dim=1).eq(1)\n    eligible = valid & ~first_valid\n    draws = torch.rand(\n        (*sequence.shape, 1), device=item_vectors.device,\n        generator=generator)\n    remove_content = eligible.unsqueeze(-1) & draws.lt(p)\n    return item_vectors.masked_fill(remove_content, 0.0)\n\n\n# Once per training process, outside the minibatch loop:\n# aug_gen = torch.Generator(device=device)\n# aug_gen.manual_seed(42)\n# Keep initialization, ordering, negative sampling, and default dropout\n# RNG streams separately controlled and matched across conditions.\n\n# Inside EXISTING SASRec log2feats:\n# original_padding_mask = sequence.eq(0)\n# x = self.item_emb(sequence) * (self.hidden_dim ** 0.5)\n# x = item_content_dropout(\n#     sequence, x, p=self.content_dropout_p,\n#     training=self.training, generator=self.aug_gen)\n# Continue baseline positional addition, ordinary dropout, padding-zeroing,\n# causal attention, residuals, normalization, and scoring in original order.\n# Every input-content residual must branch AFTER the transformation above.\n# Build attention eligibility from original IDs, never from zeroed vectors.\n# Candidate embeddings and tied embedding weights are never masked.\n\n# Checkpoint includes model/config/optimizer/scheduler and:\n# checkpoint['augmentation_rng_state'] = aug_gen.get_state()\n# Also save Python, NumPy, default CPU/CUDA, sampler/shuffler RNG states.\n# On compatible-device resume:\n# aug_gen.set_state(checkpoint['augmentation_rng_state'])\n\n# Predictor writer reconstructs configuration and calls model.eval().\n# No augmentation generator is needed on evaluation-only no-op paths.\n\n# DiagnosisProbe.run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n#     saved_modes = [(module, module.training)\n#                    for module in view.model.modules()]\n#     try:\n#         view.model.eval()\n#         with torch.no_grad():\n#             compute existing clean diagnostics\n#             optionally apply explicit deterministic embedding overrides\n#             use isolated audit RNG, fixed candidates, and original positions\n#             return {'metrics': metrics,\n#                     'metric_definitions': metric_definitions}\n#     finally:\n#         for module, previous_mode in saved_modes:\n#             module.training = previous_mode\n#         restore any additional state touched by optional audits\n\n# Compare baseline p=0 with p=0.1 within remaining shared compute budget.\n# Preserve all routes, splits, seeds, loss, sampler, and checkpoint rules.\n# Select exclusively through pinned host macro validation HR@10.\n# Stability controls remain auxiliary; no empirical improvement is claimed.",
